\section{Proofs}
\label{app:proofs}

\paragraph{Proof of \cref{prop:decouple}.}
The gradient flow of \eqref{eq:loss} with $\Wt=\sg(W)$ is
$\dot W=P^\top(W\Ctc-PW\Ccc)-\lambda W$ and $\dot P=\alpha\big((W\Ctc-PW\Ccc)W^\top-\lambda_PP\big)$.
Substituting the ansatz, $W\Ctc=\sum_jw_js_jv_ju_j^\top$ and $PW\Ccc=\sum_jp_jw_jc_jv_ju_j^\top$, so
$P^\top(W\Ctc-PW\Ccc)=\sum_jp_jw_j(s_j-p_jc_j)v_ju_j^\top$ and $(W\Ctc-PW\Ccc)W^\top=\sum_jw_j^2(s_j-p_jc_j)v_jv_j^\top$.
Both right-hand sides stay in the span of the ansatz, which is therefore invariant, and the coefficients obey \eqref{eq:wdot}--\eqref{eq:pdot}. Uniqueness of solutions of the (locally Lipschitz) flow completes the proof. \qed

\paragraph{Proof of \cref{prop:window}.}
$r_j(p)=-c_jp^2+s_jp-\lambda$ is a concave quadratic, positive between its roots $p^\pm_j=\big(s_j\pm\sqrt{s_j^2-4\lambda c_j}\big)/(2c_j)$, which are real and distinct iff $s_j^2>4\lambda c_j$.
At $(0,0)$ the Jacobian of \eqref{eq:wdot}--\eqref{eq:pdot} is $\operatorname{diag}(-\lambda,-\alpha\lambda_P)$.
A fixed point with $w\ne0$ needs $r_j(p)=0$, i.e.\ $p\in\{p^-_j,p^+_j\}$, and $w^2=\lambda_Pp/(s_j-pc_j)$, positive since $p^\pm_j<\rho_j$.
The Jacobian there is $\begin{pmatrix}0& w(s_j-2pc_j)\\ 2\alpha w(s_j-pc_j) & -\alpha(w^2c_j+\lambda_P)\end{pmatrix}$, with negative trace and determinant $-2\alpha w^2(s_j-2pc_j)(s_j-pc_j)$, which is positive iff $p>\rho_j/2=(p^+_j+p^-_j)/2$, i.e.\ at $p^+_j$ (stable) and not at $p^-_j$ (saddle). \qed

\paragraph{Proof of \cref{prop:stopgrad}.}
Without stop-gradient the loss along direction $j$ is $\tfrac12w_j^2(p_j^2c_j-2p_js_j+c^t_j)$, so $\dot w_j=-w_j(p_j^2c_j-2p_js_j+c^t_j+\lambda)=-w_j\big(c_j(p_j-\rho_j)^2+c^t_j-\rho_js_j+\lambda\big)$.
By Cauchy--Schwarz, $s_j^2=(\E[(u_j^\top x_t)(u_j^\top x_c)])^2\le c^t_jc_j$, so $c^t_j-\rho_js_j\ge0$. \qed

\paragraph{Proof of \cref{prop:masking}.}
With $b_i\sim\mathrm{Bern}(q)$ i.i.d., $x_c=Bx$, $x_t=(I-B)x$, $B=\operatorname{diag}(b)$:
$\E[x_cx_c^\top]_{il}=\E[b_ib_l]C_{il}=q^2C_{il}+q(1-q)\delta_{il}C_{ii}$ and $\E[x_tx_c^\top]_{il}=\E[(1-b_i)b_l]C_{il}=q(1-q)C_{il}(1-\delta_{il})$.
For stationary $x$, $C$ is circulant with $C_{ii}=\sigma^2$, so $\Ccc=q^2C+q(1-q)\sigma^2I$ and $\Ctc=q(1-q)(C-\sigma^2I)$, both diagonal in the Fourier basis of $C$. \qed

\paragraph{EMA target.}
\label{app:ema}
With $\dot{\bar w}_j=\kappa(w_j-\bar w_j)$ and $p$ frozen, the linearization at $w=\bar w=0$ has matrix $\begin{pmatrix}-(p^2c_j+\lambda)&ps_j\\ \kappa&-\kappa\end{pmatrix}$, whose trace is negative and whose determinant $\kappa(p^2c_j+\lambda-ps_j)$ is negative iff $r_j(p)>0$; so an unstable (growing) mode exists iff $r_j(p)>0$, with growth rate $g$ solving $g^2+g(\kappa+p^2c_j+\lambda)=\kappa r_j(p)$, hence $0<g<r_j(p)$.

\subsection{Derivation of the survival criterion}
\label{app:survival}

We derive \cref{prop:survival} for one direction and drop the index $j$.
Write $N(p)=\lambda_Pp/(s-pc)$ for $0<p<\rho$; the predictor nullcline of \eqref{eq:pdot} is $w^2=N(p)$, with $\dot p<0$ below it and $\dot p>0$ above it.

\paragraph{Phase I (below the nullcline).}
While $w^2\ll N(p)$, the signal term in \eqref{eq:pdot} is negligible against the decay term, so $p(t)=p_0e^{-\alpha\lambda_Pt}$ to leading order, and \eqref{eq:wdot} integrates to $\log w(t)=\log\varepsilon+\int_0^tr(p(t'))\,dt'$.
Since $p$ is monotone in this phase we change variables, $dt'=-dp/(\alpha\lambda_Pp)$:
\begin{align*}
\log w&=\log\varepsilon+\int_p^{p_0}\frac{sp'-cp'^2-\lambda}{\alpha\lambda_Pp'}\,dp'\\
&=\log\varepsilon+\frac{s(p_0-p)-\tfrac c2(p_0^2-p^2)-\lambda\log(p_0/p)}{\alpha\lambda_P}
=\log\varepsilon+G(p).
\end{align*}
$G$ increases while $p$ is inside the window and decreases outside it.

\paragraph{Phase II (capture).}
Phase I ends when the trajectory reaches the nullcline, $2\log w=\log N(p)$, at some $p<\rho$; this happens before time $T$ if and only if $\log\varepsilon+G(p)-\tfrac12\log N(p)$ becomes positive for some $p\in(p_T,\min(p_0,\rho))$, which is $\Phi>0$.
Above the nullcline $\dot p>0$, so $p$ moves back up towards $\rho$, through the window, where $w$ grows; the trajectory then approaches the stable fixed point $(w^\ast,p^+)$ of \cref{prop:window}, which exists by \eqref{eq:fp}.
If $\Phi\le0$, $w$ stays below the nullcline, $p$ leaves the window by the time $T$ (or never enters it), and $w$ decays at rate at least $\lambda$ once $p<p^-$.

\paragraph{Regime of validity.}
The argument makes three approximations.
(i)~In phase I the signal term is neglected up to the crossing; it is of relative size $w^2/N(p)$, so it is negligible except in a neighbourhood of the crossing whose duration is $O(1/r)$, which is short against the drift time $1/(\alpha\lambda_P)$ when $\alpha\lambda_P\lesssim\lambda\lesssim r$.
(ii)~Capture after crossing requires that $p$ return into the window faster than $w$ decays: the predictor's return rate is $\alpha w^2(s-pc)$ and $w$ decays at most at rate $\lambda$ while $p<p^-$, which favours capture when $\alpha\lesssim1$ and $\lambda_P\lesssim\lambda$; for fast predictors with strong predictor weight decay the trajectory can cross the nullcline and still fall back to collapse, and the basin must be computed numerically.
(iii)~Discrete optimization steps are replaced by the flow, which requires learning rates small against $1/(p^2c)$ and $1/(\alpha w^2c)$.

\paragraph{Numerical check.}
The scripts \texttt{survival\_criterion\_equal\_decay.py}, \texttt{survival\_criterion\_general.py} and \texttt{survival\_criterion\_depth.py} in \texttt{notes/pilot/} of the manuscript source integrate \eqref{eq:wdot}--\eqref{eq:pdot} by forward Euler (step $0.01$) from log-uniformly drawn parameters ($c\in[10^{-1.5},10^{0.5}]$, $s/c\in[0.1,1]$ uniform, $p_0\in[10^{-1},10^{0.5}]$, $\varepsilon\in[10^{-4},10^{-2}]$) and call a direction surviving if $w(T)>10^{-3}$.
With $\lambda_P=\lambda\in[10^{-3},10^{-1}]$, $\alpha=1$, $T=3000$, the criterion (phase-I integral evaluated numerically) predicts the outcome in 297 of 300 settings and \eqref{eq:fp} alone in 183.
With $\lambda\in[10^{-3},10^{-1.3}]$, $\lambda_P/\lambda$ and $\alpha$ in $[10^{-1/2},10^{1/2}]$, $T=4000$, the closed form \eqref{eq:survival} together with \eqref{eq:fp} predicts 371 of 400.
For the depth-$L$ predictor of \cref{cor:depth} ($\lambda_P=\lambda$, $\alpha=1$, $T=4000$) it predicts 149, 150 and 147 of 150 for $L=1,2,3$, against 83, 73 and 82 for \eqref{eq:fp} alone.
For the predictor learning rate (\texttt{predictor\_lr\_convention.py}; same parameter ranges, $\lambda_P=\lambda$ or $\eta_P=\lambda$, $T=4000$), the fraction of 400 settings that survive is $0.405$, $0.335$, $0.240$ for $\alpha=0.3,1,3$ with a fixed decay coefficient and $0.323$, $0.335$, $0.345$ with a fixed decay rate, the signs predicted in \cref{sec:heuristics}.
These checks test the asymptotics against the exact mode dynamics only; they say nothing about nonlinear encoders, which \cref{sec:experiments} addresses.

\paragraph{Proof of \cref{cor:depth}.}
With $P=\prod_{l}A_l$ and balanced initialization $A_l(0)=a_0\sum_jv_jv_j^\top$, all factors stay equal and diagonal in $\{v_j\}$ by the argument of \cref{prop:decouple}; the gradient of the loss with respect to one factor along direction $j$ is $a_j^{L-1}$ times that with respect to $p_j$, giving the stated $\dot a_j$.
Then $\dot p_j=La_j^{L-1}\dot a_j=L\alpha\big(p_j^{2-2/L}w_j^2(s_j-p_jc_j)-\lambda_Pp_j\big)$: at $w_j=0$ the gain decays at rate $L\alpha\lambda_P$, and the nullcline is $w_j^2=\lambda_Pp_j^{2/L-1}/(s_j-p_jc_j)$.
Repeating the derivation above with these two substitutions gives the corollary. \qed

