\paragraph{Linear BYOL/SimSiam \citep{tian2021understanding}.}
Take $s_j=1$, $c_j=1+\sigma^2$ for all $j$ (isotropic data, additive augmentation of variance $\sigma^2$), a stop-gradient target, and $\lambda=\alpha\lambda_P=\eta$.
Writing $\varsigma_j=w_j^2$, \eqref{eq:wdot}--\eqref{eq:pdot} become $\dot\varsigma_j=2\varsigma_j\big(p_j-(1+\sigma^2)p_j^2-\eta\big)$ and $\dot p_j=\alpha\varsigma_j\big(1-(1+\sigma^2)p_j\big)-\eta p_j$, the eigenmode equations of linear SimSiam in \citet{tian2021understanding} with their EMA ratio set to one, and \eqref{eq:fp} becomes their condition $\eta<1/\big(4(1+\sigma^2)\big)$ for a non-collapsed fixed point.
In this setting all directions obey the same equations and differ only through their initial conditions; with masking they differ through $s_j$ and $c_j$ (\cref{prop:masking}).

\paragraph{Weight decay as a threshold on augmentation variance \citep{wang2021demystify}.}
Let the two views be $x_c=x+\xi$ and $x_t=x+\xi'$ with independent zero-mean augmentation noise, $\E[xx^\top]=\Sigma$ and $\E[\xi\xi^\top]=\E[\xi'\xi'^\top]=N$ commuting with $\Sigma$, eigenvalues $d_j$ and $n_j$.
Then $\Ctc=\Sigma$ and $\Ccc=\Sigma+N$, so \cref{ass:codiag} holds with $s_j=d_j$, $c_j=d_j+n_j$, $\rho_j=d_j/(d_j+n_j)$, and \eqref{eq:fp} reads $n_j<d_j\big(d_j/(4\lambda)-1\big)$: weight decay discards the directions whose augmentation variance is large relative to their signal, which is the role \citet{wang2021demystify} prove for weight decay in a linear network with a DirectPred-type predictor.
Their predictor is set from the representation statistics rather than trained, so the drift of $p_j$ through the window, and hence \cref{prop:survival}, has no counterpart there.

\paragraph{No weight decay \citep{littwin2024jepa}.}
At $\lambda=\lambda_P=0$ the window of \cref{prop:window} is $(0,\rho_j)$ and does not close: $\dot p_j=\alpha w_j^2c_j(\rho_j-p_j)$ and $\dot w_j=w_jp_jc_j(\rho_j-p_j)$, so from $0<p_0<\rho_j$ every direction with $s_j>0$ grows and $p_j\to\rho_j$.
Along every learned direction the predictor converges to the regression coefficient $\rho_j$, the quantity by which \citet{littwin2024jepa} characterize the features deep linear JEPAs (trained without weight decay) learn first.
Their result concerns the order and speed of learning as a function of encoder depth, which our single-layer encoder does not resolve; our depth result (\cref{cor:depth}) concerns the predictor.

\paragraph{Stepwise learning from small initialization \citep{simon2023stepwise}.}
For $\lambda_P\to0$ the predictor stays at $p_0$ in phase~I and $w_j(t)=\varepsilon_je^{r_j(p_0)t}$, so direction $j$ reaches amplitude $w$ at $t_j=\log(w/\varepsilon_j)/r_j(p_0)$: directions are learned one at a time, at onset times logarithmic in the initialization scale and inverse in their rate, as in the stepwise picture of \citet{simon2023stepwise} (derived there for a Barlow Twins loss without a predictor).
With $\lambda_P>0$ the rate decreases along the trajectory, and the onset time is finite only if $\Phi_j>0$: a direction whose onset would come after its window closes is never learned.

\paragraph{Loss-landscape collapse thresholds \citep{ziyin2023shapes}.}
\Cref{prop:window} is the stationary-point statement for our model (with a stop-gradient the flow is not a gradient flow, so it plays the role of the landscape): the collapsed point is locally stable for every $\lambda>0$, and a non-collapsed stationary point exists along direction $j$ if and only if $\rho_js_j>4\lambda$, so the number of directions with a non-collapsed stationary point decreases with weight decay, as in the collapse thresholds that \citet{ziyin2023shapes} derive for linear SSL objectives with weight decay (the constants differ because their models differ from ours).
This count is an upper bound on the rank reached from small initialization; in the check of \cref{app:survival} it agrees with the integrated outcome in 183 of 300 settings against 297 for \cref{prop:survival}.

\paragraph{Rank differential \citep{zhuo2023rank}.}
In the linear model the online and target outputs along direction $j$ are $p_jw_j$ and $w_j$, so the two branches differ in rank whenever $p_j$ varies across directions; at the non-collapsed fixed points $p_j=p_j^+$, which increases with $s_j$ at fixed $c_j$.
We do not derive the sign of the effective-rank difference that \citet{zhuo2023rank} identify as the mechanism; it is a measured quantity in \cref{sec:experiments}.
